## 2. Interface contract — the normative schema

### 2.1 Field-by-field derivation from SAEOS-1 §4.2

Every §4.2 field is present. Four fields are **added**; each exists to make exactly one
§4.2 invariant mechanically checkable, and each is a strict tightening (see DD-1, A7).

| §4.2 field | Schema encoding | Required | Rationale / clause |
|---|---|---|---|
| `id: URI` | `^sakg://<ns>/<Type>/<ULID>$`, Crockford base32, 26 chars, first char `0-7` | both profiles | §4.2 id form; ULID uppercase is canonical |
| `type: NodeType` | closed `enum` of the 12 §4.3 types | both | §4.3 "closed set at v1"; closedness is the Concept-OS boundary (§6.2) |
| `namespace: ProjectRef` | `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,62}$` | both | Ω5 tenancy boundary; DNS-label-ish so it is safe in URIs and paths |
| `facets: {name:{...}}` | open map, snake_case keys → object values, `maxProperties: 32` | both, may be `{}` | ✓7 ECS extension point — the *only* place domain content lives |
| `label: string` | 1–120 chars | both | INDEX-tier unit |
| `summary: string` | 1–**280** chars | both | Ω3/FPL-4 — the SUMMARY-tier projection unit; the bound *is* the context-economy law |
| `provenance.origin: OriginRef` | `{system, run_id, goal_id, stage?}` | both | §6.1b "producing system + run/goal id" |
| `provenance.author_kind` | `enum(human, agent, ingest, inferred)` | both | FPL-7 |
| `provenance.ingested_at` | RFC3339 UTC timestamp | both | FPL-7 |
| `provenance.source_hash` | `sha256:<64 hex>` | both | ✓4 content-addressed source |
| `evidence: [EvidenceRef]` | array of **closed** EvidenceRef objects | both, may be `[]` | §3.2.3 references only |
| `status` | `enum(candidate, canonical, superseded, refuted)` | both; `const candidate` at `#ingest` | §6.1b "nodes land as candidate" |
| `confidence: float` | `number` in `[0,1]` | **forbidden** at `#ingest`, **required** at `#stored` | Δ6 — derived, never authored |
| `valid_from` | timestamp | both | FPL-6 |
| `valid_until` | timestamp \| `null` (null = open) | both, present-with-null | FPL-6; presence keeps serialization uniform → deterministic `corpus_hash` (§5.1 r3) |
| `falsifier` | string(1–500) \| `null` | both, present-with-null | Ω4/K6 |
| `coordinates: {dim:[value]}` | map → array **of strings**, `minItems 1`, unique | both, may be `{}` | ✓8 — string items are the structural bar on hierarchy |
| **+ `confidence_derivation`** | `{method, inputs[], computed_at, corpus_seq}`, `method` enum excludes any authored value | required iff `confidence` present; forbidden at `#ingest` | **DD-1a**, makes Δ6 checkable |
| **+ `promotion`** | `{basis, evidence_hash, promoted_at}`, `basis ∈ {outcome_observed, benchmark_bound, sealed_goal}` | required iff `canonical`; forbidden iff `candidate`; forbidden at `#ingest` | **DD-1b**, makes ×5 checkable |
| **+ `lifecycle`** | `{retained: const true, terminated_at, superseded_by?, refuted_by?}` | required iff `superseded`/`refuted`; forbidden otherwise | **DD-1c**, makes "nothing is ever deleted" checkable |
| **+ `review_priority`** | `{reference_count, distinct_namespaces, last_seen}` | optional, any status | **DD-1d** — gives frequency a *legal home that is not `promotion`*, encoding "frequency may raise review priority only" (×5) |

### 2.2 Design decisions

**DD-1 — one added field per unenforceable invariant.** §4.2's four invariants are
statements about *provenance of a value*, *reason for a transition*, and *corpus
retention*. None is expressible over a bare §4.2 document: a `float` confidence cannot say
who computed it, and `status: canonical` cannot say why. Left as-is, the invariants would
be policy, not structure — the exact failure mode §3.3 forbids — and the goal's own
acceptance criterion ("invalid fixtures exercising authored confidence, frequency-based
promotion, deleted node") would be unsatisfiable, because such a fixture would be
byte-indistinguishable from a valid one. Rejected alternative: enforce in the ingest
validator instead. Rejected because the frozen artifact would then carry no falsifier
(Δ3, K6) and the fixture corpus could not exist. Each added field is **conditionally
required and closed**, so it can only ever reject documents the base would have accepted —
a tightening, never a widening (D2). Routed as a CER-2 proposal against SAEOS-1 §4.2; it
does **not** block S2 (A7).

**DD-2 — the envelope is closed; extension happens only through `facets` and
`coordinates`.** `unevaluatedProperties: false` at both profile roots. This is
load-bearing: it is what makes a smuggled tombstone key (`"deleted": true`) a schema
failure rather than an ignored annotation, and it is the direct encoding of §4.2's "every
node carries the same envelope; type-specific content lives in facets" (✓7).

**DD-3 — composition rule (draft-2020-12 pitfall).** Every property **name** is declared
once, in `$defs/envelope_base.properties`. Profile subschemas may only *re-constrain*
existing names; they may never introduce a new one. `additionalProperties` is deliberately
**not** used at the envelope level (it does not see names contributed by a sibling `$ref`,
which would silently reject every valid document); `unevaluatedProperties` is used
instead, because its annotations do flow across `$ref`/`allOf` in 2020-12.
`additionalProperties: false` **is** used on the leaf objects (`provenance`, `origin_ref`,
`evidence_ref`, `confidence_derivation`, `promotion`, `lifecycle`, `review_priority`),
where there is no composition.

**DD-4 — `format` is an annotation, not an assertion.** Draft 2020-12 makes `format`
non-assertive by default, so a validator's configuration would decide whether a timestamp
is checked — nondeterminism inside a *frozen* artifact. Every temporal and hash field
therefore carries a `pattern` that does the asserting; `format: date-time` is retained for
tooling only. Timestamps are RFC3339 **UTC with a literal `Z`** (A9), which also makes
lexical order equal chronological order for the store.

**DD-5 — EvidenceRef is closed and has no result slot.** `additionalProperties: false` on
`evidence_ref`, and the object declares `kind, hash, uri, artifact_hash,
environment_hash?, reverification` — deliberately **no** `result` and **no** `attestation`
(contrast PAEOS-7.6 §6 `Evidence`, which has both). This is §3.3.3 "no evidence minting"
made structural: SAKG cannot express a verdict, only a pointer plus re-verification
instructions.

**DD-7 — prohibitions are `not/required` branches, never `false` subschemas.** The obvious
way to forbid a field is `"properties": {"confidence": false}`. Smoke-testing the schema
(§2.4) showed this is a diagnostic dead end: a boolean `false` subschema reports at the
*document root* with no keyword and a schema pointer truncated to `.../properties`, so two
different prohibitions are indistinguishable in the error stream and a fixture cannot be
pinned to the rule it is supposed to violate. Each prohibition is therefore its own
`allOf` branch of the form `{"not": {"required": ["<field>"]}}`, which rejects at its own
addressable pointer. This is what makes the §6.2 rejecting-clause column meaningful.

**DD-6 — required-but-possibly-empty.** `facets`, `coordinates`, `evidence`, `valid_until`
and `falsifier` are **required**, with `{}`/`[]`/`null` as the empty forms, rather than
optional. Uniform key presence gives every node one canonical serialization, which is what
lets §5.1's `corpus_hash`/`query_hash` be stable and a projection be reproducible.

