### 2.4 Pre-flight verification of the schema text

The §2.3 text was not just written — it was **executed in-design**, in memory, against
representative documents for every fixture family, so that IMPLEMENT starts from a schema
already known to work. This is design-stage self-check, **not** `IMPLEMENT` or `VERIFY`
evidence: no file was created under `sakg/` or `tests/`, and the §11 acceptance commands
were not run.

What was established:

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `Draft202012Validator.check_schema()` | valid draft 2020-12 |
| All 18 `$ref` targets resolve; no unreferenced `$defs` | clean |
| DD-3 composition rule — every name constrained by a profile is declared in `envelope_base` | holds |
| Representative valid documents for the ingest, canonical, high-frequency-candidate, superseded, refuted and decayed families | all accept |
| All 16 invalid families | all reject, each at a distinct, addressable clause (§6.2) |

Two defects were found this way and are fixed in the text above rather than left for
IMPLEMENT to discover:

1. **Prohibitions were unaddressable.** `"confidence": false` rejected at the document root
   with a truncated schema pointer, making two different prohibitions indistinguishable in
   the error stream — which would have made T3's anti-false-positive control vacuous for the
   ingest-profile fixtures. Fixed by DD-7 (`not/required` branches).
2. **The invalid-fixture expectation was anchored to the wrong axis.** Instance paths
   collapse to `()` for every root-level keyword, which is most of the corpus. Fixed by
   re-anchoring §6.2 and T3 to `absolute_schema_path`.

Residual: this exercised *representative* documents, not the 32 authored fixture files —
authoring them is IMPLEMENT's job, and T2/T3 are what prove them.

