## 10. Perspectives covered (multi-perspective critique input)

- **Correctness / conformance.** Every §4.2 field appears in §2.1 and in the schema text;
  every §4.2 invariant maps to a schema clause in §5; T4/T5/T6 make coverage a *test*
  rather than a claim; and §2.4 records that the schema text was executed rather than
  merely asserted — which is how DD-7 was found. ✔
- **Falsifiability (Δ3, K6).** SAEOS-1 requires every spec to state at least one mechanically
  checkable conformance assertion. This design states 32 (the fixture corpus) plus 8 test
  predicates, and §9's assumptions each carry a falsifier. ✔
- **Non-authority (FPL-3, §3.3).** Three structural expressions, not three rules:
  EvidenceRef has no result slot (cannot mint evidence); `#ingest` pins `status: candidate`
  (a producer cannot promote); `promotion.basis` admits only evidence bindings (popularity
  cannot promote). ✔
- **Poisoning resistance (Ω6).** The corpus's cheapest attack is mass low-quality
  assertion to manufacture consensus. The schema makes that attack *inert*: frequency can
  only ever land in `review_priority`, which is structurally disjoint from `promotion`, so
  a flood raises a review queue and changes nothing a consumer sees under the default
  `status: [canonical]` filter. ✔
- **Context economy (FPL-4, Ω3).** `summary ≤ 280`, `label ≤ 120`, and bounded
  `maxItems`/`maxProperties` throughout mean a node's SUMMARY projection has a computable
  worst-case size — the budget in §5.1 is enforceable rather than aspirational. ✔
- **Tenancy (Ω5).** `namespace` is required and pattern-bounded on every node from day one,
  so an untenanted node is unrepresentable even before S5 exists. ✔
- **Decay / Truth Maintenance (FPL-6, Δ6).** `valid_from`/`valid_until`/`falsifier` are
  required (present-with-null), and no confidence can be recorded without naming its method
  and inputs — §4.3's recompute loop has a schema-checkable footprint. ✔
- **Extensibility (✓7, ✓8).** The envelope is closed and `facets`/`coordinates` are open.
  Adding a domain costs a facet key; it never costs envelope surgery. Widening later is
  cheap, narrowing later would be a rewrite — the §9 A1 trade, taken deliberately. ✔
- **Round-trip fidelity (Ω1).** Fixture 03 is the Scar round-trip probe. If PAEOS's scar
  bundle cannot serialize into facets losslessly, fixture 03 is where it shows — which is
  A8's load-bearing S1 assumption getting its first test. ✔
- **Cost / proportionality.** One inert JSON file, 32 small fixtures, one test module, one
  test-only dependency. Nothing added to the runtime path; the schema can be deleted with
  zero correctness impact on PAEOS (×6). ✔
- **Adversarial (what would break this).** The strongest attack on the design is DD-1: if
  ratification rejects the four added fields, three of the four invariants lose their
  schema teeth. A7 states the fallback (registered facet keys) explicitly, and the fallback
  preserves every check at a different path. The residual is field naming, not enforcement. ✔

